      "body": "Confirmed: you still have the same 2 uncommitted template files, and `main` hasn't been merged into `downstream/flare_forecast` yet. Here are the commands, in order \u2014 you already have this same working-tree state from before, so it's the identical stash/pop pattern, just with a merge step in between instead of a checkout-and-back:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Stash your uncommitted template edits so the merge has a clean working tree\ngit stash push -m \"wip: template s3_cache_dir + notebook tweaks\"\n\n# 2. Make sure you're on your feature branch (should already be there)\ngit checkout downstream/flare_forecast\n\n# 3. Merge main into it\ngit merge main\n\n# 4. Bring your stashed edits back\ngit stash pop\n```\n\nWhat to expect at each step:\n\n- **Step 3** is the one that might not go cleanly \u2014 `main` touched `workshop_infrastructure/configs.py`, `workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py`, `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py`, and `downstream_apps/template/configs/config_script.yaml`. If your branch's changes overlap those, git will open the standard conflict markers (`<<<<<<<` / `=======` / `>>>>>>>`) in the affected files and pause the merge \u2014 it won't auto-resolve or discard anything. If that happens, stop and show me the conflicting files rather than guessing at a resolution.\n- **Step 4** (stash pop) could also conflict if `main`'s merge touched the same 2 template files you have edits stashed \u2014 if so, git will tell you and leave the stash intact (not dropped) so nothing is lost; you'd resolve those markers too.\n\nIf step 3 conflicts, don't run step 4 yet \u2014 finish resolving and committing the merge first, then pop the stash separately. Want me to run step 1\u20133 now and report back what happens, or would you rather run them yourself and paste me the output?"
